{"ratings":[{"id":"C1","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A successful targeted gate can mark a criterion met without evidence that every requested test actually ran."},{"id":"C2","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A quoted test path is dropped during parsing, allowing an unrelated passing suite to satisfy the criterion."},{"id":"C3","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Gate setup can alter tracked source so tests pass, after which the factory attributes the results to the restored failing commit."},{"id":"C4","consequence":"resume","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A crash between saving gate results and completing the stage causes resume to reuse the results while permanently rejecting their evidence."},{"id":"C2b","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A passing test command can mark a multi-step criterion met even when another required verification step remains blocked."}]}